1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:01,860
April fifteenth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,420
Meera deposits fifteen thousand rupees at

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,100
the bank.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,460
Her phone buzzes.

5
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:09,060
Your account is credited with fifteen thousand

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:09,860
rupees.

7
00:00:10,706 --> 00:00:11,706
Credited!

8
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:15,266
Credit means money in, and money in is good.

9
00:00:15,426 --> 00:00:16,066
Right?

10
00:00:17,113 --> 00:00:18,513
Khata has news.

11
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:21,593
In Meera's own books, that same deposit

12
00:00:21,673 --> 00:00:22,713
is a debit.

13
00:00:24,116 --> 00:00:25,636
So who's right?

14
00:00:31,578 --> 00:00:33,778
First, last time's questions.

15
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:34,778
One.

16
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:37,178
The four thousand rupee catering advance.

17
00:00:37,258 --> 00:00:38,778
Is it April's revenue?

18
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:40,536
No.

19
00:00:40,736 --> 00:00:43,456
The stall still owes that customer an event.

20
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,656
It's a liability.

21
00:00:45,211 --> 00:00:45,771
Two.

22
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:48,611
Paying Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

23
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:50,171
Is that an expense?

24
00:00:51,322 --> 00:00:51,722
No.

25
00:00:52,122 --> 00:00:54,522
Paying Gopal just shrinks what the stall

26
00:00:54,602 --> 00:00:55,322
owes him.

27
00:00:55,893 --> 00:00:56,573
Three.

28
00:00:56,853 --> 00:00:59,653
After Infotech pays four thousand, how much

29
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:00,933
do they still owe?

30
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:03,282
Two thousand.

31
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:06,242
Six thousand billed, four thousand paid.

32
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,350
Now, that SMS.

33
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,670
First, four myths to drop.

34
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:13,550
Debit isn't bad.

35
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:15,430
Credit isn't good.

36
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:18,790
Neither one means money in, or money out.

37
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:21,510
And neither one means plus, or minus.

38
00:01:22,147 --> 00:01:24,227
So here's what they really mean.

39
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:27,187
In accounting, debit just means left.

40
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:29,707
And credit just means right.

41
00:01:30,628 --> 00:01:33,268
Remember Khata's two pages, from the very

42
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:34,228
first lesson?

43
00:01:34,468 --> 00:01:35,428
This is why.

44
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:38,159
So which side do you write on?

45
00:01:38,639 --> 00:01:39,679
Look at the scale.

46
00:01:39,919 --> 00:01:41,239
Assets on the left.

47
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:43,599
Liabilities and equity on the right.

48
00:01:43,999 --> 00:01:46,559
Every account has a home side: the side

49
00:01:46,639 --> 00:01:47,599
where it lives.

50
00:01:48,155 --> 00:01:49,515
And here's the whole rule.

51
00:01:49,995 --> 00:01:52,635
When an account grows, write it on its home

52
00:01:52,715 --> 00:01:53,235
side.

53
00:01:54,035 --> 00:01:56,595
When it shrinks, write it on the other side.

54
00:01:57,644 --> 00:01:58,924
April first.

55
00:01:59,004 --> 00:02:01,484
Meera puts in fifty thousand rupees.

56
00:02:01,604 --> 00:02:03,084
Which two things changed?

57
00:02:05,051 --> 00:02:05,851
Cash went up.

58
00:02:05,931 --> 00:02:08,331
An asset, home on the left.

59
00:02:08,731 --> 00:02:09,931
Debit Cash.

60
00:02:10,671 --> 00:02:11,791
Capital went up.

61
00:02:12,031 --> 00:02:13,871
Equity, home on the right.

62
00:02:14,031 --> 00:02:15,191
Credit Capital.

63
00:02:15,391 --> 00:02:18,031
Fifty thousand left, fifty thousand right.

64
00:02:18,786 --> 00:02:21,826
Now the cart, thirty-six thousand rupees.

65
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:23,666
Which two things changed?

66
00:02:25,618 --> 00:02:27,018
Equipment grows.

67
00:02:27,218 --> 00:02:29,418
An asset, home on the left.

68
00:02:29,618 --> 00:02:30,898
Debit Equipment.

69
00:02:31,798 --> 00:02:33,438
But Cash shrinks.

70
00:02:33,638 --> 00:02:36,278
Cash lives on the left, so to shrink it,

71
00:02:36,358 --> 00:02:37,358
write on the right.

72
00:02:37,558 --> 00:02:38,918
Credit Cash.

73
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:43,026
So debit doesn't always mean up.

74
00:02:44,046 --> 00:02:45,406
It depends on the home.

75
00:02:46,672 --> 00:02:48,912
Liabilities work the same way, from the

76
00:02:48,992 --> 00:02:49,872
other side.

77
00:02:49,952 --> 00:02:51,952
Gopal Dairy sends eight thousand rupees

78
00:02:52,032 --> 00:02:53,712
of stock, pay later.

79
00:02:53,792 --> 00:02:54,992
Stock grows.

80
00:02:55,072 --> 00:02:56,272
Debit Stock.

81
00:02:56,352 --> 00:02:57,312
And Gopal?

82
00:02:57,392 --> 00:02:58,832
Debit, or credit?

83
00:03:00,393 --> 00:03:01,113
Credit.

84
00:03:01,353 --> 00:03:04,713
What the stall owes Gopal grew, and liabilities

85
00:03:04,873 --> 00:03:05,833
live on the right.

86
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,020
April fifth.

87
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:10,260
Rent, five thousand rupees.

88
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:11,740
Cash shrinks.

89
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:12,940
Credit Cash.

90
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:14,400
And Rent?

91
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:15,960
Rent grew.

92
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:17,640
But where is its home?

93
00:03:18,388 --> 00:03:20,068
Remember the long equation?

94
00:03:20,308 --> 00:03:23,108
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

95
00:03:23,228 --> 00:03:25,668
plus revenue, minus expenses.

96
00:03:26,388 --> 00:03:28,268
That minus is the clue.

97
00:03:28,348 --> 00:03:30,828
Move Expenses across the equals sign, and

98
00:03:30,868 --> 00:03:32,668
the minus becomes a plus.

99
00:03:33,224 --> 00:03:36,504
Assets, plus expenses, equal liabilities,

100
00:03:36,624 --> 00:03:38,744
plus capital, plus revenue.

101
00:03:40,324 --> 00:03:43,044
So expenses live on the left, with the assets.

102
00:03:43,284 --> 00:03:45,924
Rent grew, and the left is its home.

103
00:03:46,084 --> 00:03:47,364
Debit Rent.

104
00:03:48,024 --> 00:03:49,704
Every expense is a debit.

105
00:03:50,469 --> 00:03:53,269
Revenue never moved, so its home stays on

106
00:03:53,349 --> 00:03:54,949
the right, with capital.

107
00:03:55,189 --> 00:03:57,189
Every sale is a credit.

108
00:03:58,257 --> 00:03:59,057
Put it together.

109
00:03:59,537 --> 00:04:02,737
Debits live on the left: Expenses, Assets,

110
00:04:02,777 --> 00:04:05,377
and Drawings, the money Meera takes home.

111
00:04:05,457 --> 00:04:07,137
They behave like expenses.

112
00:04:07,686 --> 00:04:10,366
Credits live on the right: Liabilities,

113
00:04:10,566 --> 00:04:13,446
Income, that's revenue, or income, same

114
00:04:13,566 --> 00:04:14,886
thing, and Capital.

115
00:04:15,366 --> 00:04:16,886
In short: DEAD CLIC.

116
00:04:17,366 --> 00:04:18,646
You didn't memorise this.

117
00:04:18,806 --> 00:04:20,886
You read it straight off the equation.

118
00:04:21,452 --> 00:04:24,252
And in every transaction, the left total

119
00:04:24,492 --> 00:04:25,932
equals the right total.

120
00:04:26,252 --> 00:04:26,972
Always.

121
00:04:27,692 --> 00:04:30,132
That's double entry: every rupee written

122
00:04:30,212 --> 00:04:32,532
twice, once on each side.

123
00:04:32,612 --> 00:04:34,932
The same scale, in a new costume.

124
00:04:36,178 --> 00:04:38,178
So, the SMS.

125
00:04:38,738 --> 00:04:40,978
Two cameras, one deposit.

126
00:04:41,058 --> 00:04:43,618
In Meera's books, money moved from the galla

127
00:04:43,698 --> 00:04:44,658
to the bank.

128
00:04:44,738 --> 00:04:45,938
Bank grows.

129
00:04:45,978 --> 00:04:46,978
Debit Bank.

130
00:04:47,178 --> 00:04:48,178
Cash shrinks.

131
00:04:48,338 --> 00:04:49,458
Credit Cash.

132
00:04:50,022 --> 00:04:52,342
In the bank's books, the notes land in its

133
00:04:52,422 --> 00:04:53,142
vault.

134
00:04:53,222 --> 00:04:54,582
That's its debit.

135
00:04:54,662 --> 00:04:57,062
But Meera's fifteen thousand isn't the bank's

136
00:04:57,142 --> 00:04:57,622
money.

137
00:04:57,862 --> 00:05:00,262
It's money the bank owes her, any day she

138
00:05:00,342 --> 00:05:00,942
asks.

139
00:05:01,142 --> 00:05:03,422
For the bank, that's a liability.

140
00:05:03,622 --> 00:05:06,182
It grows, so the bank credits it.

141
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:07,460
Same money.

142
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:09,060
Opposite books.

143
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,700
Your SMS is reading the bank's khata, not

144
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:13,420
yours.

145
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:15,500
Credit doesn't mean good.

146
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:17,500
Debit doesn't mean bad.

147
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:19,740
They just mean right, and left.

148
00:05:21,076 --> 00:05:22,036
Let's practise.

149
00:05:22,196 --> 00:05:24,716
Warm-up first: one account at a time.

150
00:05:24,916 --> 00:05:25,716
Which side?

151
00:05:26,036 --> 00:05:27,916
Ravi Mama's loan grows.

152
00:05:29,516 --> 00:05:30,156
Credit.

153
00:05:30,396 --> 00:05:31,396
Rent grows.

154
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:33,596
Debit.

155
00:05:33,796 --> 00:05:34,556
A sale.

156
00:05:36,156 --> 00:05:36,636
Credit.

157
00:05:37,036 --> 00:05:37,916
Now, careful.

158
00:05:38,156 --> 00:05:40,956
Infotech pays what it owes, so the Infotech

159
00:05:41,036 --> 00:05:42,316
account shrinks.

160
00:05:44,276 --> 00:05:44,916
Credit.

161
00:05:45,156 --> 00:05:47,476
An asset shrinking goes on the right.

162
00:05:48,223 --> 00:05:49,423
Now full entries.

163
00:05:49,823 --> 00:05:51,143
Khata goes first.

164
00:05:51,423 --> 00:05:53,823
Cash sales for the first half of April:

165
00:05:53,903 --> 00:05:55,383
eighteen thousand rupees.

166
00:05:55,663 --> 00:05:57,343
Which two things changed?

167
00:05:59,314 --> 00:06:00,034
Cash grew.

168
00:06:00,114 --> 00:06:02,834
Debit Cash, eighteen thousand.

169
00:06:02,914 --> 00:06:03,954
Sales grew.

170
00:06:04,034 --> 00:06:06,594
That's revenue, so credit Sales, eighteen

171
00:06:06,674 --> 00:06:07,554
thousand.

172
00:06:07,634 --> 00:06:09,154
Left equals right.

173
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:10,710
Meera's turn.

174
00:06:11,190 --> 00:06:13,990
Infotech's chai tab: six thousand rupees,

175
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:14,950
pay later.

176
00:06:15,190 --> 00:06:17,910
The chai was sold, so credit Sales, six

177
00:06:18,070 --> 00:06:18,950
thousand.

178
00:06:19,030 --> 00:06:20,070
Now Infotech.

179
00:06:20,470 --> 00:06:21,750
They owe the stall.

180
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:23,910
Meera slides it toward the right page.

181
00:06:24,310 --> 00:06:26,910
Owing money, that was Gopal's side.

182
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:27,830
Wasn't it?

183
00:06:30,178 --> 00:06:30,578
No.

184
00:06:30,978 --> 00:06:32,818
Gopal is owed by the stall.

185
00:06:33,298 --> 00:06:35,098
Infotech owes the stall.

186
00:06:35,298 --> 00:06:36,978
An asset, and it grew.

187
00:06:37,378 --> 00:06:38,818
Home side, left.

188
00:06:39,058 --> 00:06:41,218
Debit Infotech, six thousand.

189
00:06:42,135 --> 00:06:43,455
Now, just you.

190
00:06:43,735 --> 00:06:46,695
Meera pays Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

191
00:06:46,935 --> 00:06:47,695
Debit what?

192
00:06:47,895 --> 00:06:48,775
Credit what?

193
00:06:51,941 --> 00:06:53,381
Debit Gopal Dairy.

194
00:06:53,861 --> 00:06:56,261
The liability shrinks, so it goes on the

195
00:06:56,461 --> 00:06:56,901
left.

196
00:06:57,141 --> 00:06:58,581
Credit Cash.

197
00:06:58,661 --> 00:07:00,501
Five thousand leaves the galla.

198
00:07:01,757 --> 00:07:02,477
So.

199
00:07:02,557 --> 00:07:05,037
Debit is left, credit is right.

200
00:07:05,157 --> 00:07:07,277
Grow an account on its home side, shrink

201
00:07:07,357 --> 00:07:08,477
it on the other.

202
00:07:08,517 --> 00:07:11,117
And the bank's SMS speaks from the bank's

203
00:07:11,357 --> 00:07:11,917
books.

204
00:07:13,126 --> 00:07:13,766
Your turn.

205
00:07:13,926 --> 00:07:14,926
Three quick ones.

206
00:07:15,126 --> 00:07:15,606
One.

207
00:07:15,846 --> 00:07:18,566
Meera buys six thousand rupees of tea, milk

208
00:07:18,646 --> 00:07:20,446
and sugar, and pays cash.

209
00:07:20,646 --> 00:07:22,646
What's debited, and what's credited?

210
00:07:23,206 --> 00:07:23,686
Two.

211
00:07:23,926 --> 00:07:26,006
Why is an expense a debit?

212
00:07:26,326 --> 00:07:26,886
Three.

213
00:07:27,126 --> 00:07:29,206
Imagine Meera pays a bill from the bank,

214
00:07:29,286 --> 00:07:31,126
and the SMS says debited.

215
00:07:31,526 --> 00:07:34,486
In Meera's books, is Bank debited, or credited?

216
00:07:34,966 --> 00:07:36,646
Answers next lesson.

217
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:40,627
Next time, Meera's CA friend drops by the

218
00:07:40,707 --> 00:07:42,467
stall, with rules.

219
00:07:42,707 --> 00:07:43,747
Debit the receiver!

220
00:07:43,907 --> 00:07:44,987
Credit the giver!

221
00:07:45,187 --> 00:07:47,187
Is that a whole new system to learn?
